    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23807v1</id>
    <title>Steering beyond Language: Improving Multilingual Training-Free Text Embeddings via Paired-Residual Subspace Removal</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T17:30:43Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23807v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23807v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>While deriving text embeddings directly from frozen LLMs has emerged as a promising direction, existing methods focus almost exclusively on English, leaving multilingual settings largely unexplored. In this work, we present the first systematic study of multilingual training-free text embeddings in LLMs. Our analysis reveals a language-conditioned embedding geometry in which semantically equivalent sentences remain misaligned across languages. We hypothesize that language-specific variation can be disentangled from semantic content to improve embedding quality. To this end, we propose Paired-Residual Subspace Removal (PRSR), a training-free inference-time intervention that steers LLM representations toward a more language-invariant semantic space. PRSR first constructs paired residuals from multilingual parallel sentences to capture language-specific deviations. It then estimates layer-wise low-rank subspaces from these residuals and suppresses the corresponding components in the last-token hidden states. We conduct more than 200 monolingual evaluations across 18 languages using MTEB and MMTEB, along with cross-lingual bitext mining between English and over 200 other languages. Empirical results demonstrate that PRSR consistently improves the performance of existing training-free baselines in both monolingual and cross-lingual settings across different LLMs.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2026-10-16T17:30:43Z</published>
