    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23530v1</id>
    <title>EduWild: How Teacher-Configured Chatbots Fail in Real-World Use and How to Fix Them</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T14:28:06Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23530v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23530v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Teachers increasingly configure educational chatbots through natural-language instructions, but how these bots fail in real-world use remains poorly understood. We introduce EduWild, a dataset of 1,726 conversations with 217 teacher-configured chatbots, each paired with the instructions and configuration behind it, including 900 conversations from the intended end users of 35 deployed chatbots. We develop a 29-code taxonomy covering factual, workflow, diagnostic, and pedagogical failures and apply it using a human-calibrated LLM judge. The most frequent failures are lapses in following teacher intent --- bots skip required workflow steps (53.8% of end-user conversations), omit expectation setup (33.4%), and overload learners with information or questions (37.1%). Guided by these findings, we design a prompt-based harness targeting the six most frequent failure modes. In a study with 200 college students across ten bots, the harness reduces the mean number of target failure codes per conversation from 1.406 to 0.489, a 65.2% relative reduction with bots weighted equally. These findings show how real-world failure analysis can guide practical improvements to educational chatbots.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
