    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23521v1</id>
    <title>Perceive, Solve, Reason: Progressive Capability Formation in Time Series-LLMs</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T14:23:23Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23521v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23521v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models (LLMs) can now perceive, describe, and reason about images and audio, yet time series understanding remains underestimated: most work uses LLMs as forecasters and classifiers, reasoning studies are few and narrow, and no suite trains and evaluates the full range of capabilities. In this paper, we build the first such suite, with 337K training and 44K evaluation samples across three progressive capabilities, Perceive, Solve, and Reason, and use it to ask how time series should enter an LLM that must master all three, and in what order. We find that a dedicated encoder is essential: with one, a 4B model surpasses GPT-4o on every perception attribute we test, whereas textualizing the series costs 8--10 points in reasoning accuracy. The capabilities then build on one another: learning them in order beats mixing the same data and rivals reasoning-only training with over three times the reasoning data, but only when an encoder is used. Analyses further reveal that capabilities are transferred through distinct network depths rather than simply stacked, and that a stage converged on its own benchmark is not necessarily ready for the next. Distilled into a simple recipe, these findings yield 4B TS-LLMs that, without any target-domain data, outperform four recent 7B TS-LLMs on seven of eight out-of-domain metrics, by up to 11 points. We therefore advocate treating time series as a first-class modality for LLMs, read through dedicated encoders and taught to perceive before they reason.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
