    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23470v1</id>
    <title>QD-Rec: Open-Ended Evolution of Recommendations via Quality-Diversity</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T13:49:22Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23470v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23470v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Open-ended search combined with foundation models has produced advances such as faster algorithms, new results on open mathematical problems, and self-improving agents. We extend this paradigm to recommendation for decision-making, focusing on decisions where the set of possible choices is fixed but the reasons for choosing them are not. In high-stakes decisions, e.g., which company to acquire or which customer to target, comparing diverse, well-reasoned options can be more useful than a single ranked list. We formalize open-ended recommendation as building a diverse, high-quality set of recommendations, each an item together with a hypothesis, a natural-language reason why the item fits the needs of a decision maker. We propose QD-Rec, a quality--diversity method that selects items with a contextual bandit and generates hypotheses with a foundation model from earlier ones kept in an archive as stepping stones. A new recommendation enters the archive if it is novel or better than its nearest neighbor. Across M&amp;A, customer targeting, and paper and book recommendation, QD-Rec gives the highest mean quality and builds the largest archive in most settings.</summary>
    <category term="cs.IR" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
