  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23400v1</id>
    <title>On the Tip of the Tongue: First-Token Cueing as a Behavioral Probe of Recall Failures in LLMs</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T12:55:36Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23400v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23400v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Large language models often answer factual questions incorrectly in closed-book settings, and such errors are usually attributed to missing knowledge. Yet models often encode answers they fail to produce. We show that many closed-book errors are first-token recall failures: the model commits to a wrong first token, but once given the right one it completes the answer and often recognizes it as correct. We study this with First-Token Cueing, a behavioral probe that prefills the response with the first token of the gold answer. Model uncertainty peaks at the first answer token, where it already predicts correctness, and among gold prefixes of any length the first token alone accounts for the largest share of recovered errors. Across 16 open models, this one token corrects between a fifth and a half of the errors on multi-token answers in English QA and improves accuracy in all seven languages of a multilingual benchmark. Most of the errors it corrects are also solved by the model's own sampled reasoning, a criterion for encoded knowledge, and models recognize correct starts they do not produce: they continue a correct start more confidently than an incorrect start of the same answer type and prior probability, yet rarely find it without the gold answer. These results locate a recoverable class of factual errors at the first decoding step, where inference-time methods may gain the most.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
