    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23309v1</id>
    <title>Inferring L1 Backgrounds from L2 English with LLM Token Probabilities</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T11:12:42Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23309v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23309v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Native language identification (NLI) infers a non-native speaker's first language (L1) from their second language (L2) production. We investigate a token probability approach that scores each text sample as a continuation of a prompt prefix specifying a candidate L1, then predicts the candidate with the highest score. We evaluate our approach using Llama 3.1 8B and 70B on three datasets: TOEFL9, Write and Improve 2024, and BULATS-L1. We compare its performance with previous prompting-based classification approaches. Token-probability scoring substantially improves on direct classification, particularly with the 8B model, while the 70B model achieves state-of-the-art performance across all datasets. Our results indicate that pretrained LLMs' token probabilities contain information useful for distinguishing L1 backgrounds from L2 English production.</summary>
