    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23226v1</id>
    <title>Characterising and Analysing Retrieval-Augmented Generation Techniques</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T09:55:31Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23226v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23226v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>The advent of Large Language Models (LLMs) across many application domains has increased the need for Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) to reduce hallucinations and improve LLM performance. Thus far, several RAG techniques with impressive features and capabilities have been proposed in the literature, making it a herculean task to select the most appropriate RAG techniques for specific problem scenarios. Thus, we propose a conceptual framework comprising main dimensions and sub-dimensions for characterising (defining "what it is") and analysing (defining "how well it does") existing RAG techniques. The conceptual framework was developed through a qualitative analysis of the attributes of RAG techniques reported in the literature. It can serve as a pre-empirical filtering mechanism for researchers and practitioners in selecting the highest-fit RAG candidates that meet specific user and task requirements before empirical benchmarking of RAG techniques, which can be computationally and financially expensive.</summary>
    <category term="cs.AI" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
