    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23185v1</id>
    <title>A Stronger Teacher Within: Strengthening MoE Models via On-Policy Bidirectional Distillation</title>
    <updated>2026-10-16T09:23:22Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23185v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2610.23185v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) has substantially improved Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) LLMs, but its outcome-level rewards provide only sparse supervision. On-Policy Distillation offers dense token-level feedback, yet requires a stronger compatible teacher, while on-policy self-distillation relies on hand-designed privileged information. We observe that the MoE architecture itself offers a way to resolve this dilemma, as increasing the activation budget per token exposes latent expert capacity and yields a potentially stronger teacher within the model. However, naive expansion degrades performance because newly co-activated experts are not optimized to work together. We therefore propose MoEvolve, a bidirectional distillation framework following a recovery-then-improvement strategy: we first restore the activation-expanded high-$k$ model and further strengthen it by exploiting its additional active capacity, then reverse the direction of distillation to transfer the enhanced capabilities back into the original low-$k$ configuration, retaining its inference-time activation budget. Across diverse MoE models and benchmarks, MoEvolve improves the initial models by up to $22.2$ domain-average points and outperforms same-budget RLVR baselines by up to $6.5$ points. Notably, the final low-$k$ models often surpass their activation-expanded teachers.</summary>
    <category term="cs.LG" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
